/**
  @page examples-pdes-helmholtz Solving the Helmholtz equation
  @brief Solve a complex wave problem and check it against a manufactured solution.

  This example extends the @ref examples-pdes-poisson "Poisson workflow" to a
  complex-valued wave amplitude. It introduces a negative mass term, explains
  why conjugate gradients is unsuitable here, and exports the real and imaginary
  parts of the computed field separately.

  @tableofcontents
  @m_footernavigation

  @section examples-pdes-helmholtz-intro The mathematical problem

  On @f$\Omega=(0,1)^2@f$, consider
  @f[
    -\Delta u-k^2u=f\quad\text{in }\Omega,
    \qquad u=u_{\mathrm{ex}}\quad\text{on }\partial\Omega,
    \qquad k=4\pi.
  @f]
  The complex amplitude may represent a time-harmonic field; a corresponding
  real oscillation is @f$\operatorname{Re}(u e^{-i\omega t})@f$. This is a
  bounded Dirichlet problem, not an outgoing-wave or absorbing-boundary model.

  A manufactured solution makes the source and boundary data explicit:
  @f[
    u_{\mathrm{ex}}(x,y)=e^{ikx}\sin(\pi y),\qquad
    f(x,y)=\pi^2u_{\mathrm{ex}}(x,y).
  @f]
  Indeed, @f$-\Delta u_{\mathrm{ex}}=(k^2+\pi^2)u_{\mathrm{ex}}@f$.
  The chosen @f$k^2@f$ is not a Dirichlet eigenvalue of the unit square.

  @section examples-pdes-helmholtz-weak The weak formulation

  For complex test functions with zero boundary trace, the weak equation is
  @f[
    \int_\Omega\nabla u\cdot\overline{\nabla v}\,dx
       -k^2\int_\Omega u\overline v\,dx
       =\int_\Omega f\overline v\,dx.
  @f]
  The bar denotes complex conjugation. Rodin's complex forms are linear in the
  trial field and conjugate-linear in the test field. The negative mass term
  makes this system indefinite, so the positive-definiteness requirement of CG
  is not satisfied.

  @section examples-pdes-helmholtz-mesh 1. Resolve the wave on a mesh

  The program generates a unit-square triangular mesh with 97 vertices in each
  coordinate direction: 9409 vertices and 18432 triangles. The wavelength in the
  horizontal direction is @f$2\pi/k=0.5@f$.

  @snippet PDEs/Helmholtz.cpp helmholtz-mesh

  Resolving a wavelength is necessary but does not by itself control wave
  dispersion or proximity to resonance. Refinement is still needed to assess
  the error for the frequency of interest.

  @section examples-pdes-helmholtz-problem 2. Define the complex problem

  `P1<Complex>` stores a complex coefficient at each mesh vertex. Both the source
  and the boundary values come from the same manufactured solution.

  @snippet PDEs/Helmholtz.cpp helmholtz-form

  As in the Poisson example, the load is subtracted to express a residual.
  Here the additional negative mass term represents the time-harmonic wave term.

  @section examples-pdes-helmholtz-solve 3. Solve the indefinite system

  Sparse LU is used for this modest two-dimensional demonstration. It does not
  require a positive definite matrix. The program checks solver success before
  exporting the result.

  @snippet PDEs/Helmholtz.cpp helmholtz-solve

  @section examples-pdes-helmholtz-output 4. Inspect real and imaginary parts

  The output stores two real-valued fields, avoiding ambiguity when opening a
  complex field in a visualization application.

  @snippet PDEs/Helmholtz.cpp helmholtz-export

  @image html PDEs/Helmholtz.png "Computed real and imaginary amplitudes, using the same signed color scale." width=720px

  Open `Helmholtz.xdmf` in ParaView, select **Apply**, then color by **real** or
  **imaginary**. Keep the HDF5 files beside the XDMF file. The reference fields
  for comparison are @f$\cos(kx)\sin(\pi y)@f$ and
  @f$\sin(kx)\sin(\pi y)@f$.

  @section examples-pdes-helmholtz-full Complete program

  @snippet PDEs/Helmholtz.cpp helmholtz-source

  See @ref PDEs/Helmholtz.cpp "Helmholtz.cpp" for the source and the
  @ref guides-getting-started-installation "installation guide" for build setup.
  The optional plotting script in `examples/PDEs/visualize.py` reproduces the
  figure from the exported point values.

  @example PDEs/Helmholtz.cpp @m_examplenavigation{examples-pdes-helmholtz,PDEs/} @m_footernavigation
*/
